FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/runtime.rs:2535-2540 — a `pop_left` with an empty front reverses the whole back stack through `deque_reverse` (src/runtime.rs:2129-2133), leaving all remaining items on the front; the next `pop_right` finds an empty back and reverses the whole front back (src/runtime.rs:2559-2563). Alternating pops at opposite ends therefore does a full O(n) rebuild on every other pop, O(n²) for n pops — exactly the workflow a deque exists for, and worse than the `skip` cost the phase compares against. `edge_a_deque_beats_a_list_work_queue_at_the_front` only ever pops `pop_left`, so it never exercises this path. Concrete fix: on rebalance, split the non-empty stack so both ends retain roughly half the items (banker's-queue rebalancing), or otherwise avoid reversing the whole remainder; add a test that alternates `pop_left`/`pop_right` over a large deque and asserts node sharing/cost. — VERIFIED: traced the two pop arms to `deque_reverse`, which calls `stack_pop_order` over the entire stack and `stack_from_values` to rebuild it; the only performance test (tests/collections_test.rs:517-620) performs one direction only.

2. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:1925-1931 — `deque_parts` validates only each spine tree's root via `stack_tree_parts`, and `stack_tree_parts` (src/runtime.rs:1982-2006) checks the immediate children's declared `size` fields but never recurses into those children. A hand-written record whose top node has plausible sizes but a malformed nested node is accepted by `push_left`/`push_right`/`pop_*` and only refused on a later operation when the nested node becomes the root. That contradicts the current REPORT's claim that “Every operation validates both ends and every tree on each spine before returning a deque,” and leaves the malformed-nested-node branch untested (tests/collections_test.rs:282-317 only forge malformed top-level trees). Concrete fix: validate each node recursively (or iteratively over the whole tree) in `stack_tree_parts`, and add a forged nested-node test.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
